+++
title: Susa
description: Ancient city in Iran
hook: In ca. 1175 BC the Elamites under Shutruk-Nahhunte plundered the stele
  bearing the Code of Hammurabi and carried it home to Susa. Archeologists
  found it there in 1901.
standfirst: An ancient city in the lower Zagros Mountains of Iran, about 250
  km (160 mi) east of the Tigris and set between the Karkheh and Dez Rivers.
  Susa was the capital of Elam and the winter capital of the Achaemenid
  Empire, and it stayed a strategic centre under the Parthians and Sasanians.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Shush, Khuzestan Province, Iran
    from: "location: Shush, Khuzestan Province, Iran"
  - name: The setting
    value: The lower Zagros Mountains, between the Karkheh and Dez Rivers, about
      250 km (160 mi) east of the Tigris
    from: was an ancient city in the lower [[ Zagros Mountains ]] about 250 km
      (160 mi) east of the [[ Tigris ]], between the [[ Karkheh |
      karkheh-river ]] and [[ Dez | dez-river ]] Rivers in [[ Iran ]]
  - name: Settled
    value: As early as 4200 BC, based on calibrated carbon-14 dating
    from: Based on calibrated [[ carbon-14 dating ]], the foundation of a
      settlement there occurred as early as 4200 BC.[[ ^42 ]]
  - name: Abandoned
    value: 1218 AD
    from: "abandoned: 1218 AD"
  - name: Capital of
    value: Elam, and the winter capital of the Achaemenid Empire
    from: Susa served as the capital of [[ Elam ]] and the winter capital of the
      [[ Achaemenid Empire ]]
  - name: World Heritage listing
    value: Inscribed by UNESCO in July 2015
    from: In July 2015, it was inscribed on the list of [[ World Heritage Sites |
      world-heritage-site ]] by [[ UNESCO ]].[[ ^109 ]]
pull-quotes:
  - text: '"Susa, the great holy city, abode of their gods, seat of their
      mysteries, I conquered. I entered its palaces, I opened their treasuries
      where silver and gold, goods and wealth were amassed. . . .I destroyed
      the [[ ziggurat ]] of Susa. I smashed its shining copper horns. I
      reduced the temples of Elam to naught; their gods and goddesses I
      scattered to the winds. The tombs of their ancient and recent kings I
      devastated, I exposed to the sun, and I carried away their bones toward
      the land of Ashur. I devastated the provinces of Elam and, on their
      lands, I sowed salt."'
    attribution: Ashurbanipal, on a tablet unearthed in 1854 by Austen Henry
      Layard in Nineveh
    section: neo-assyrians
  - text: '"This palace which I built at Susa, from afar its ornamentation was
      brought. Downward the earth was dug, until I reached rock in the earth.
      When the excavation had been made, then rubble was packed down, some 40
      cubits in depth, another part 20 cubits in depth. On that rubble the
      palace was constructed."'
    attribution: Darius the Great, in an inscription describing his new capital
    section: achaemenid-period
  - text: '"the Susanians are participating entirely in an Uruk way of life. They
      are not culturally distinct; the material culture of Susa is a regional
      variation of that on the Mesopotamian plain"'
    attribution: Holly Pittman, an art historian at the University of Pennsylvania
      in Philadelphia
    section: susa-ii-and-uruk-influence-38003100-bc
  - text: '"An expansion once thought to have lasted less than 200 years now
      apparently went on for 700 years. It is hard to think of any colonial
      system lasting that long. The spread of Uruk material is not evidence of
      Uruk domination; it could be local choice"'
    attribution: Gilbert Stein, director of the University of Chicago's Oriental Institute
    section: susa-ii-and-uruk-influence-38003100-bc
  - text: '"bulk clearings more like removing earthworms than conducting true
      scientific excavations."'
    attribution: Laurianne Martinez-Sève, classical archeologist, on Roland de
      Mecquenem's methods
    section: excavation-history
timeline:
  - when: 4200 BC
    what: A settlement is founded at Susa — as early as this, on calibrated
      carbon-14 dating.
    from: Based on calibrated [[ carbon-14 dating ]], the foundation of a
      settlement there occurred as early as 4200 BC.[[ ^42 ]]
  - when: c. 4200–3900 BC
    what: The Susa I period. Two settlements, the Acropolis (7 ha) and the Apadana
      (6.3 ha), would later merge to form Susa proper (18 ha).
    from: Susa's earliest settlement is known as the //Susa I// period (c.
      4200–3900 BC). Two settlements named by archaeologists the //Acropolis//
      (7 ha) and the //Apadana// (6.3 ha), would later merge to form Susa
      proper (18 ha).[[ ^42 ]]
  - when: 3100–2700 BC
    what: Susa III, also known as the 'Proto-Elamite' period; the Proto-Elamite
      tablets first appear in the record.
    from: Susa III (3100–2700 BC) is also known as the '[[ Proto-Elamite ]]'
      period.[[ ^61 ]] At this time, Banesh period pottery is predominant.
      This is also when the Proto-Elamite tablets first appear in the record.
  - when: 2700 BC
    what: A battle between Kish and Susa is recorded, when En-me-barage-si is said
      to have "made the land of Elam submit".
    from: A battle between [[ Kish | kish-sumer ]] and Susa is recorded in 2700
      BC, when [[ En-me-barage-si ]] is said to have "made the land of Elam
      submit".[[ ^62 ]]
  - when: approximately 2330 BC
    what: Sargon the Great incorporates Susiana into his Akkadian Empire.
    from: Susiana was incorporated by [[ Sargon the Great ]] into his [[ Akkadian
      Empire ]] in approximately 2330 BC.[[ ^66 ]]
  - when: ca. 2100 BC
    what: Kutik-Inshushinak, governor of the Akkadian province, rebels and makes
      Susa an independent state and a literary center.
    from: Susa was the capital of an [[ Akkadian | akkadian-empire ]] province
      until ca. 2100 BC, when its governor, [[ Kutik-Inshushinak ]], rebelled
      and made it an independent state and a literary center.
  - when: ca. 2004 BC
    what: Ur collapses at the hands of the Elamites under Kindattu; Susa is ruled
      by Elam again and becomes its capital under the Shimashki dynasty.
    from: The city was subsequently conquered by the neo-Sumerian [[ Third Dynasty
      of Ur ]] and held until Ur finally collapsed at the hands of the
      Elamites under [[ Kindattu ]] in ca. 2004 BC. At this time, Susa was
      ruled by Elam again and became its capital under the Shimashki dynasty.
  - when: Around 1500 BC
    what: The Middle Elamite period begins with the rise of the Anshanite
      dynasties, and an "Elamisation" of Susa.
    from: Around 1500 BC, the Middle Elamite period began with the rise of the
      Anshanite dynasties. Their rule was characterized by an "Elamisation" of
      Susa, and the kings took the title "king of Anshan and Susa".
  - when: ca. 1175 BC
    what: The Elamites under Shutruk-Nahhunte plunder the stele bearing the Code
      of Hammurabi and take it to Susa.
    from: In ca. 1175 BC, the Elamites under [[ Shutruk-Nahhunte ]] plundered the
      original [[ stele ]] bearing the //[[ Code of Hammurabi ]]// and took it
      to Susa.
  - when: 647 BC
    what: The Neo-Assyrian king Ashurbanipal levels the city; Assyrian rule of
      Susa begins.
    from: In 647 BC, [[ Neo-Assyrian ]] king [[ Ashurbanipal ]] leveled the city
      during a war in which the people of Susa participated on the other side.
  - when: 617 BC
    what: The Median capture of Susa ends Assyrian rule.
    from: Assyrian rule of Susa began in 647 BC and lasted till [[ Median | medes
      ]] capture of Susa in 617 BC.
  - when: between 540 and 539 BC
    what: Cyrus the Great captures Susa; the city becomes part of the Persian
      Achaemenid empire.
    from: Susa underwent a major [[ political ]] and [[ ethnocultural ]]
      transition when it became part of the Persian [[ Achaemenid ]] empire
      between 540 and 539 BC when it was captured by [[ Cyrus the Great ]]
      during his conquest of [[ Elam ]] (Susiana), of which Susa was the
      capital.[[ ^83 ]]
  - when: 472 BC
    what: The Persians, the Athenian tragedy set in Susa and the oldest surviving
      play in the history of theatre.
    from: The city forms the setting of //[[ The Persians ]]// (472 BC), an [[
      Athenian | classical-athens ]] [[ tragedy ]] by the [[ ancient Greek |
      ancient-greece ]] playwright [[ Aeschylus ]] that is the oldest
      surviving play in the [[ history of theatre ]].
  - when: 324 BC
    what: Alexander meets Nearchus at Susa, and celebrates a mass wedding there
      between the Persians and Macedonians.
    from: In 324 BC, Alexander met [[ Nearchus ]] here, who explored the Persian
      Gulf as he returned from the Indus River by sea. In that same year
      Alexander celebrated in Susa with a [[ mass wedding | susa-weddings ]]
      between the [[ Persians ]] and [[ Macedonians | ancient-macedonians
      ]].[[ ^91 ]]
  - when: Around 147 BC
    what: Susa and the adjacent Elymais break free from the Seleucid Empire.
    from: Around 147 BC Susa and the adjacent [[ Elymais ]] broke free from the [[
      Seleucid Empire ]].
  - when: 116 AD
    what: The Roman emperor Trajan briefly captures Susa during his Parthian
      campaign — the furthest east the Roman Empire ever advanced.
    from: Susa was briefly captured in 116 AD by the Roman emperor [[ Trajan ]]
      during the course of his [[ Parthian campaign |
      trajans-parthian-campaign ]].[[ ^99 ]] Never again would the Roman
      Empire advance so far to the east.[[ ^100 ]]
  - when: 224 AD
    what: The Sassanid Ardashir I conquers and destroys Susa; it is rebuilt
      immediately thereafter.
    from: Susa was conquered and destroyed in 224 AD by the Sassanid [[ Ardashir I
      ]], but rebuilt immediately thereafter, and perhaps even temporarily a
      royal residence.
  - when: "339"
    what: After a rebellion of Christians living in Susa, Shapur II destroys the
      city using 300 elephants.
    from: Following a rebellion of Christians living in Susa, the king destroyed
      the city in 339 using 300 elephants.[[ ^102 ]]
  - when: "642"
    what: An Arab army under Abu Musa al-Ash'ari captures Tustar and goes on to
      besiege Susa.
    from: After taking most of the smaller fortified towns the army captured [[
      Tustar | shushtar ]] in 642 before proceeding to besiege Susa.
  - when: "1218"
    what: Invading Mongols raze the city, which never regains its previous
      importance.
    from: In 1218, the city was razed by invading [[ Mongols ]] and was never able
      to regain its previous importance.
  - when: July 2015
    what: Susa is inscribed on the list of World Heritage Sites by UNESCO.
    from: In July 2015, it was inscribed on the list of [[ World Heritage Sites |
      world-heritage-site ]] by [[ UNESCO ]].[[ ^109 ]]
