glossary:
  - term: Susiana
    gloss: The state Susa was capital of in the Sumerian period, occupying
      approximately the same territory as modern Khūzestān Province, centered
      on the Karun.
    from: In the [[ Sumerian | sumer ]] period, Susa was the capital of a state
      called !!Susiana!! (Šušan), which occupied approximately the same
      territory of modern [[ Khūzestān Province ]] centered on the [[ Karun
      River | karun ]].
  - term: kudurru
    gloss: A Babylonian boundary stone; de Morgan found a collection of them at
      Susa.
    from: a collection of Babylonian [[ kudurrus | kudurru ]] (boundary stones)
  - term: spolia
    gloss: Bricks taken from the ancient site itself and reused; Shush Castle was
      built largely out of them.
    from: a European medieval style castle on the northernmost edge of the
      Acropole, which was made largely out of [[ spoliated | spolia ]] bricks
      taken from the ancient site itself
  - term: Ensi
    gloss: Governor — the title Puzur-Inshushinak held at Susa.
    from: '"[[ Puzur-Inshushinak ]] [[ Ensi | ensi-sumerian ]] (Governor) of Susa"'
  - term: Linear Elamite
    gloss: A script Kutik-Inshushinak encouraged the use of, and which remains
      undeciphered.
    from: He encouraged the use of the [[ Linear Elamite ]] script, that remains
      undeciphered.
  - term: Seleucia on the Eulaeus
    gloss: The name the city carried under the Seleucids, also given as Seleucia
      ad Eulaeum.
    from: The city was named !!Seleucia on the Eulaeus!! or !!Seleucia ad
      Eulaeum!!.
  - term: dajjal
    gloss: An Islamic term for a false messiah, compatible to the Antichrist in
      Christianity; in everyday use, it also means "deceiver" or "imposter".
    from: A //dajjal// is an Islamic term for an //[[ Al-Masih ad-Dajjal ]]//, a
      false messiah, compatible to the [[ Antichrist ]] in Christianity. In
      everyday use, it also means "deceiver" or "imposter".
  - term: Eran-Khwarrah-Shapur
    gloss: "\"Iran's glory [built by] Shapur\" — the name Shapur II gave Susa
      after having it rebuilt."
    from: He renamed it //Eran-Khwarrah-Shapur// ("Iran's glory [built by]
      Shapur").[[ ^104 ]]
further:
  - slug: chogha-zanbil
    why: The political and religious complex 30 km (19 mi) south-east of Susa,
      where the Middle Elamite policy of imposing the Elamite pantheon in
      Susiana reached its height.
  - slug: uruk
    why: Susa came within the Uruk cultural sphere, and whether it was a colony of
      Uruk or a regional variation of it is still argued over.
  - slug: persepolis
    why: Darius the Great's major building program ran at Susa and Persepolis
      together.
anchors:
  hook: In ca. 1175 BC, the Elamites under [[ Shutruk-Nahhunte ]] plundered the
    original [[ stele ]] bearing the //[[ Code of Hammurabi ]]// and took it
    to Susa. Archeologists found it in 1901.
  standfirst: "!!Susa!! ([[ /ˈsuːsə/ |
    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]] //SOO-sə//) was an
    ancient city in the lower [[ Zagros Mountains ]] about 250 km (160 mi)
    east of the [[ Tigris ]], between the [[ Karkheh | karkheh-river ]] and [[
    Dez | dez-river ]] Rivers in [[ Iran ]]. It represents the current city of
    [[ Shush | shush-iran ]],[[ ^3 ]] located on the site of ancient Susa.[[
    ^4 ]] One of the most important cities of the [[ Ancient Near East ]],
    Susa served as the capital of [[ Elam ]] and the winter capital of the [[
    Achaemenid Empire ]], and remained a strategic centre during the [[
    Parthian | parthian-empire ]] and [[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]]
    periods."
