== Elamites

In the [[ Sumerian | sumer ]] period, Susa was the capital of a state called !!Susiana!! (Šušan), which occupied approximately the same territory of modern [[ Khūzestān Province ]] centered on the [[ Karun River | karun ]]. Control of Susiana shifted between [[ Elam ]], Sumer, and [[ Akkad | akkadian-empire ]].

During the [[ Elamite monarchy | list-of-elamite-kings ]], many riches and materials were brought to Susa from the plundering of other cities.[[ ^64 ]] This was mainly due to the fact of Susa's location on Iran's South Eastern region, closer to the city of Babylon and cities in Mesopotamia.

The use of the Elamite language as an administrative language was first attested in texts of ancient Ansan, Tall-e Mal-yan, dated 1000 BC.[[ ^65 ]] Previous to the era of Elamites, the Akkadian language was responsible for most or all of the text used in ancient documents. Susiana was incorporated by [[ Sargon the Great ]] into his [[ Akkadian Empire ]] in approximately 2330 BC.[[ ^66 ]]

The main goddess of the city was [[ Nanaya ]], who had a significant temple in Susa.[[ ^67 ]]

== Old Elamite period (c. 2700–1500 BC)

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Dynastic_list_Awan_Siwashi_Louvre_Sb17729.jpg/250px-Dynastic_list_Awan_Siwashi_Louvre_Sb17729.jpg" height="153" width="122"

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Awan_Kings_List_Sb_17729_%28transcription%29.jpg/120px-Awan_Kings_List_Sb_17729_%28transcription%29.jpg" height="154" width="112"

Dynastic list of twelve kings of Awan dynasty and twelve kings of the
[[ Shimashki Dynasty ]], 1800–1600 BC, Susa, [[ Louvre Museum ]] Sb
17729.[[ ^68 ]][[ ^69 ]]

The [[ Old Elamite period ]] began around 2700 BC. Historical records mention the conquest of Elam by [[ Enmebaragesi ]], the [[ Sumerian | sumer ]] king of [[ Kish | kish-sumer ]] in [[ Mesopotamia ]]. Three dynasties ruled during this period. Twelve kings of each of the first two, those of [[ Awan | awan-dynasty ]] (or //Avan//; c. 2400–2100 BC) and [[ Simashki | shimashki-dynasty ]] (c. 2100–1970 BC), are known from a list from Susa dating to the [[ Old Babylonian period | first-babylonian-dynasty ]]. Two Elamite dynasties said to have exercised brief control over parts of Sumer in very early times include Awan and [[ Hamazi ]]; and likewise, several of the stronger [[ Sumerian | sumer ]] rulers, such as [[ Eannatum ]] of [[ Lagash ]] and [[ Lugal-anne-mundu ]] of [[ Adab | adab-city ]], are recorded as temporarily dominating Elam.

== Kutik-Inshushinak

Susa was the capital of an [[ Akkadian | akkadian-empire ]] province until ca. 2100 BC, when its governor, [[ Kutik-Inshushinak ]], rebelled and made it an independent state and a literary center. He was also the last from the [[ Awan dynasty ]] according to the Susa kinglist.[[ ^70 ]] He unified the neighbouring territories and became the king of [[ Elam ]], and he encouraged the use of the [[ Linear Elamite ]] script, that remains undeciphered.

The city was subsequently conquered by the neo-Sumerian [[ Third Dynasty of Ur ]] and held until Ur finally collapsed at the hands of the Elamites under [[ Kindattu ]] in ca. 2004 BC. At this time, Susa was ruled by Elam again and became its capital under the Shimashki dynasty.

== Indus-Susa relations (2400–2100 BC)

Numerous artifacts of [[ Indus Valley civilization ]] origin have been found in Susa from this period, especially seals and [[ etched carnelian beads ]], pointing to [[ Indus-Mesopotamia relations ]] during this period.[[ ^71 ]][[ ^72 ]]

